No, QOS Portable is not restricted to Linux hosts. It can be built and run on any host that supports the QOS Portable ABI, including macOS, FreeBSD, and Linux. The build process uses the `qos-portable.mk` script to determine the target architecture and host, allowing the application to run on a variety of platforms.

```sol
// Example of a QOS Portable application built for macOS
import qos/portable;

main =
  let x = 42 in
  qos.portable.run(x);
```

No. A send is a blocking operation that blocks the sender until the receiver has processed the message. The sender must yield control and retry the send until the receiver is ready.

```fprisc
send {
  // Blocks until receiver processes message
  // Yield here to retry
  send {
    // Process message
  }
}
```

The call returns Err "dead actor". This occurs because the `receive` function waits for a reply from the target actor, and if the actor has already died, the `receive` operation fails immediately.

```fprisc
main =
  me = myself 0;
  _ = send me (Ok "hello");
  _ = Sys.sleepUs 30000000;
  _ = receiveFrom me me;
  _ = drop me;
  "dead actor".
```

